<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oh! Susanna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">written <a href="/years/1846">1846</a>, first performed <a href="/years/1847">1847</a>, published <a href="/years/1848">1848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stephen Foster wrote &quot;Oh! Susanna&quot; in Cincinnati, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, in 1846. A local quintet first sang it at a concert in Andrews' Eagle Ice Cream Saloon in Pittsburgh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, on 11 September 1847, and W. C. Peters &amp; Co. of Cincinnati published it in 1848. Minstrel troupes made it a great success.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The writer and musician Glenn Weiser has suggested that Foster drew on two songs published in 1846: the melody of the verse resembles Billy Whitlock's &quot;Mary Blane&quot;, the opening of the chorus is almost the same as that of Silas S. Steele's &quot;Rose of Alabama&quot;, and both &quot;<b>Oh!</b></blockquote><a href="/songs/oh-susanna">Oh! Susanna</a></li></ul></section>
